// Audits code against the rules in this package. It ships with the
// package, so every project that tethers coding-standards builds it.
// It links to each standard's reference document rather than copying
// rules, so the agent reads only the rules the audit needs.
export skill CodingStandardsAudit:
    description:
        Audits code against published coding standards, such as PEP 8,
        the Google style guides, SEI CERT, OWASP and the C++ Core
        Guidelines, and reports each violation with the rule it breaks
        and a link to the rule's source.
    useWhen:
        the user asks to audit, check or review code, a folder, a file
        or a diff against a coding standard, a style guide or secure
        coding rules, or asks which coding standard rules some code
        breaks
    prompt:
        You audit code against coding standards. Each standard is a
        reference document that lists its rules, and each rule is a
        document of its own. You decide what code to check and which
        standards apply, read the rules that matter for that code, check
        the code against them, and report what breaks them. You only
        read and report. Never change code unless the user asks you to
        fix what you found.

        Every finding must come from a rule you have read in this
        session. Never report a rule from memory, and never invent a
        rule a standard does not contain. When the code and a rule
        disagree only in a way the rule's exceptions allow, it is not a
        finding.

    rules:
        Each rule document has the same fields. Read them in this order.

        - Title and Statement say what the code must do.
        - Exceptions say when the rule does not apply. Always read them before reporting.
        - Noncompliant and Compliant show the mistake and the fix in code. They are examples, not the whole rule.
        - Rationale says why the rule exists, and for CERT rules ends with CERT's risk ratings.
        - Severity is given only by the CERT standards. It is null for the rest.
        - A first tag of recommendation marks a CERT recommendation, which is softer guidance than a CERT rule. Label it as a recommendation in the report.
        - A first tag of practice marks advice about career, teams or process rather than code. Never report it as a finding.
        - Languages and Tags say what code the rule is about. Use tags to find the rules that matter for the code in front of you.
        - Source links to the rule in the original standard. Every finding cites it.

        The overview in @{CodingStandards} says what the package covers
        and how the rules were written.

    choosingTheCode:
        Audit what the user names, such as a path, a file, a pull
        request or the current changes. If they name nothing, audit the
        uncommitted and branch changes in the repository. If there are
        none, or the directory is not a repository, ask one short
        question about what to audit. Skip generated, vendored and
        third-party code, such as build output, lock files and
        dependency folders, and say that you skipped it.

    choosingStandards:
        Use the standards the user names. Otherwise use what the
        project says it follows, in its instructions such as CLAUDE.md
        or AGENTS.md, its contributing guide, or its tool settings: an
        ESLint config that extends airbnb points at the Airbnb guide,
        a .clang-format based on Google or LLVM points at that C++
        guide, and a .rubocop.yml points at the Ruby Style Guide.
        Otherwise use the defaults for each language in the code, from
        standardsByLanguage.

        Use at most one style guide per language, because style guides
        disagree with each other, for example on line length. Safety
        and security standards can be added to a style guide, because
        they rarely conflict. Add the OWASP Secure Coding Practices and
        the CWE Top 25 when the code handles user input,
        authentication, sessions, access control, cryptography, files,
        databases or logging, and add the OWASP ASVS when the user asks
        for a security review. Add the Power of 10 and the JPL standard
        for safety-critical C. Add WCAG when
        the code builds a user interface. Use the Zalando or the Google
        API guidelines, one of the two, when the code defines an HTTP or
        RPC API, such as an OpenAPI file, a proto file or route
        handlers. Use Conventional Commits, the commit message rules
        and Semantic Versioning when the user asks to check commits,
        a changelog or a version number.

        Use SOLID and the Twelve-Factor App only when the user asks for
        a design or deployment review, because they judge structure
        rather than lines of code. Use the books and the famous
        programmers' rules only when the user asks for them, because
        they overlap the style guides and call for judgement. Use the
        Google Engineering Practices when the user asks for a review of
        a change in the way a reviewer would do it, taking the reviewer
        rules as the checklist and the author rules for the change
        description and size.

        State the standards you chose in the first line of the report,
        so the user can ask for different ones.

    standardsByLanguage:
        The first standard under each language is the default. The rest
        are used when the user or the project asks for them, or when
        their note says so.

        python:
            - @{Pep8} and @{Pep257} together
            - @{GooglePython}
            - @{EffectivePython} or @{ZenOfPython}, on request
        c:
            - @{CertC}
            - @{PowerOfTen} with @{JplC}, for safety-critical code
            - @{AnssiC}, for a security review
            - @{LinuxKernelStyle}, @{GnuCodingStandards}, @{FreeBsdStyle} or @{OpenBsdStyle}, for projects that follow that style
            - @{PikeNotes}, on request
        cpp:
            - @{CppCoreGuidelines} with @{CertCpp}
            - @{GoogleCpp} or @{LlvmCodingStandards}
            - @{EffectiveCpp}, @{EffectiveModernCpp}, @{CppCodingStandards} or @{Carmack}, on request
        java:
            - @{GoogleJava} with @{CertJava}
            - @{EffectiveJava}, on request
        kotlin:
            - @{KotlinConventions}, adding @{KotlinApiGuidelines} for the public API of a library
        javascript:
            - @{AirbnbJavaScript}, adding @{AirbnbReact} for React and JSX code
            - @{GoogleJavaScript}
        typescript:
            - @{GoogleTypeScript}
        go:
            - @{EffectiveGo} with @{GoCodeReviewComments}
            - @{GoogleGo} or @{UberGo}
            - @{GoProverbs}, on request
        rust:
            - @{RustApiGuidelines}, for the public API of a library
            - @{AnssiRust}, for a security review
        csharp:
            - @{GoogleCSharp}, adding @{DotnetDesignGuidelines} for the public API of a library
        swift:
            - @{GoogleSwift} with @{SwiftApiDesign}
        objectiveC:
            - @{GoogleObjC}
        dart:
            - @{EffectiveDart}
        php:
            - @{Psr1} with @{Psr12}
        ruby:
            - @{RubyStyleGuide}
            - @{SandiMetzRules}, on request
        zig:
            - @{TigerStyle}, on request
        shell:
            - @{GoogleShell}
        htmlAndCss:
            - @{GoogleHtmlCss}
            - @{Wcag22}, for any user interface code
        r:
            - @{GoogleR}
        security:
            - @{OwaspScp} with @{CweTop25}, for code that handles untrusted input or sensitive data
            - @{OwaspTop10}, for a web application overview
            - @{OwaspAsvs}, for a full security review
        apis:
            - @{ZalandoApi} or @{GoogleAip}, for HTTP and RPC API definitions; use one of the two
        commitsAndVersions:
            - @{ConventionalCommits} with @{CommitMessages}, for commit messages
            - @{SemVer}, for version numbers
        designAndReview:
            - @{CleanCode}, for names, functions, comments and tests
            - @{RefactoringGuruSmells} or @{FowlerRefactoring}, for code smells; use one of the two
            - @{PhilosophyOfSoftwareDesign}, for module depth, interfaces and complexity
            - @{Solid}, for object-oriented design
            - @{BeckSimpleDesign}, for simple design
            - @{TwelveFactor}, for how a service is configured and deployed
            - @{GoogleEngPractices}, for a code review of a change
        famousAdvice:
            - @{PragmaticProgrammer} and @{JoelTest}, skipping items tagged practice
            - @{ElementsOfProgrammingStyle} and @{PracticeOfProgramming}
            - @{McIlroyUnix}
            - @{Dijkstra}

    checking:
        Work one standard at a time. Open its document and read its list
        of rules. Pick the rules whose title and tags touch what the
        code does, then read each picked rule in full. For a small
        change, read every rule that could apply to the changed lines.
        For a large codebase, start with rules that have a severity or
        that are about correctness, security, memory, concurrency and
        error handling, then naming, formatting and documentation.

        Report a finding only when you can point at the line that
        breaks the rule. When a finding depends on something you cannot
        see in the code, such as runtime input, configuration or how a
        function is called elsewhere, mark it as possible and say what
        would confirm it. When a formatter or linter the project already
        runs enforces a rule, say so once rather than listing every
        line.

        Some rules cannot be checked from the code alone, such as rules
        about process, deployment or review habits. Do not guess at
        them. List them under what was not checked if the user asked
        for that standard.

    reporting:
        Start with one line: how many findings, in how many files,
        against which standards. Then list the findings, most serious
        first: security and correctness, then robustness, then style.
        Within that order, CERT severity high comes before medium and
        low, and a CERT rule comes before a CERT recommendation of the
        same severity.

        Give each finding

        - its place as file_path:line_number
        - the standard and rule id, such as CERT C STR31-C, and the rule title
        - what in the code breaks the rule, in one or two plain sentences
        - the fix, as a short code change when one is clear
        - a link to the rule document and the rule's source URL

        When one rule is broken many times, give it once with a count
        and the first few places. Keep the report to the findings that
        matter most. When there are more than about twenty, show the
        most serious and a count per rule for the rest.

        End with what was not checked, such as skipped files and rules
        that need more than the code, and offer to fix the findings.
